    "description": "Village councils are elected bodies within Iran’s local-government framework. Their representative and supervisory role is distinct from the executive work of the dehyar, or village administrator.",
    "tags": ["Village councils", "Rural governance", "Dehyari", "Community participation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:42:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:42:21Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Village Councils in Iran: Rural Representation and Administration",
      "description": "Explore the distinction between village councils and dehyars, their legal responsibilities and the limits of available evidence on rural participation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "rural-representation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The village as a unit of representation",
        "content": "Article 100 of Iran’s Constitution includes villages among the localities whose affairs are supervised by elected councils. The constitutional model connects local needs with public participation, while leaving councils’ precise jurisdiction to legislation. A village council therefore has a formal representative role within the national administrative system. [s1]\n\nThe consolidated councils law identifies village-council duties in Article 102. These include following up council resolutions, proposing responses to local needs and selecting a qualified dehyar for a formal appointment order from the bakhshdar, the district administrator. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "council-and-dehyari",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Council, administrator and executive office",
        "content": "Two Persian terms help distinguish responsibilities. The dehyar is the village administrator; the dehyari is the administrative institution through which executive work is undertaken. Research on community institutions in the Karkheh River Basin describes the dehyar as working under village-council supervision. It distinguishes these formal bodies from cooperatives and informal groups organized around shared work or resources. [s3]\n\nThe council and executive office should consequently be considered separately when examining a project. Community representation, administrative execution and collective action by residents can all contribute, but they are different institutional relationships. The Karkheh research provides a local example of this distinction rather than a description of every Iranian village. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "participation-in-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation is more than an institutional title",
        "content": "The Karkheh study reported that all councils in its studied villages had male members, although some women occasionally attended meetings. It also documented women’s participation in informal collective activities. The contrast matters: contributing labor or attending a meeting is different from holding an elected position and participating in formal decisions. These findings belong to the studied communities and period; they cannot establish contemporary national representation. [s3]\n\nThe report also described variation in councils’ effectiveness associated with local social and economic conditions. Its evidence supports examining institutions in their local setting, rather than assuming that a common legal structure produces uniform results. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessing-local-governance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Questions that connect institutions to outcomes",
        "content": "A useful account of rural governance should identify who raised a need, which body made a decision and who carried out the work. It should also distinguish statutory supervision from demonstrated influence over delivery. The legal framework and the Karkheh case study support those distinctions, but neither supplies a current national evaluation. [s2][s3]\n\nFor a particular village, publication-ready reporting would require recent council records and evidence from residents, including people whose participation may be less visible in formal proceedings. That work remains necessary before drawing conclusions about present-day inclusion or service quality.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
